<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/e-abzu"><span class="enc-name">E-abzu</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The E-abzu, the house of the abzu, was the temple of the god Enki at Eridu in southern Mesopotamia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/e-babbar"><span class="enc-name">E-babbar</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The E-babbar, 'white house' in Sumerian, was the name of the temples of the sun god Utu, called Shamash in Akkadian, at Larsa and at Sippar in Mesopotamia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/e-d-morel"><span class="enc-name">E. D. Morel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">E.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/e-e-evans-pritchard"><span class="enc-name">E. E. Evans-Pritchard</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sir Edward Evan Evans-Pritchard (1902–1973) was a British social anthropologist and professor at Oxford.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/e-m-forster"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/e-m-forster.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">E. M. Forster</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">E.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ea-gamil"><span class="enc-name">Ea-gamil</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ea-gamil was the last king of the First Sealand Dynasty, which ruled the marshlands of southern Mesopotamia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ea-mukin-zeri"><span class="enc-name">Ea-mukin-zeri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ea-mukin-zeri was a king of Babylon in the Second Sealand Dynasty who seized the throne after the murder of Simbar-Shipak around 1008 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ea-nasir"><span class="enc-name">Ea-nasir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ea-nasir was a merchant of Ur around 1750 BCE who imported copper by way of Dilmun.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eadberht-of-northumbria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eadberht-of-northumbria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eadberht of Northumbria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eadberht (died 768) was king of Northumbria from 737 to 758, ruling in partnership with his brother Ecgbert, archbishop of York.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eadberht-praen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eadberht-praen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eadberht Præn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eadberht Præn was king of Kent from 796 to 798, leading a revolt against Mercian rule after the death of Offa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eadgyth"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eadgyth.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eadgyth</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eadgyth, also known as Edith of England, was a daughter of King Edward the Elder of the English and the first wife of Otto I, king of East Francia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/eadred"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/eadred.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Eadred</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Eadred (c. 923–955) was king of the English from 946 to 955, a son of Edward the Elder and younger brother of Æthelstan and Edmund.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encycl